export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"civilcraft","addon":"land-records","name":"Land records and field walk","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"land-records-start","scope":"addon:land-records","title":"Land records: getting started","summary":"Keep a record for each parcel, check the drawing against what the register says, find overlaps and gaps, subdivide, consolidate or rezone as proposals, and fill application and amendment outlines.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["IFC","BIM model","MarkupCraft","markup","BCF","building footprint","coverage","floor area ratio","takeoff","quantities","notes","markup","zoning minimum","lot size","jurisdiction","cadastre","cadastral","land records","parcel","lot","register","title","deed","reconcile","reconciliation","overlap","gap","sliver","subdivide","subdivision","consolidate","merge lots","boundary adjustment","rezoning","land use","zoning amendment","plan amendment","application","permitting","planning","schedule of parcels","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"records","text":"Records","level":2},{"id":"drawing-against-record","text":"Drawing against record","level":2},{"id":"change-parcels","text":"Change parcels","level":2},{"id":"buildings-and-quantities","text":"Buildings and quantities","level":2},{"id":"buildings-from-an-ifc-model","text":"Buildings from an IFC model","level":2},{"id":"notes-on-the-plan","text":"Notes on the plan","level":2},{"id":"mark-up-in-markupcraft","text":"Mark up in MarkupCraft","level":2},{"id":"place-rules","text":"Place rules","level":2},{"id":"documents","text":"Documents","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitch the add-on on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E, then open the \u003Cstrong\u003ELand records\u003C/strong\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ERecords\u003C/strong\u003E. Every parcel in the drawing is listed with its drawn area. Click a parcel, type what the register says about it (its number, the holder, the land use and zone, the area the record states and where that figure comes from), and press \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd the record\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003EDrawing against record\u003C/strong\u003E and press \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the drawing against the records\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003EChange parcels\u003C/strong\u003E to work out a subdivision, a consolidation, a moved boundary or a change of land use or zone as a proposal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003EDocuments\u003C/strong\u003E to fill an application or amendment outline from a proposal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EParcels come from CivilCraft&#039;s own \u003Cstrong\u003EParcel\u003C/strong\u003E tool, from deed calls, from a traverse, from a field walk (see the other topic), or from a GIS polygon layer: under \u003Cstrong\u003ERecords\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EParcels and records from a GIS layer\u003C/strong\u003E makes a parcel and a record from each polygon, taking the number, holder, land use, zone and area from the fields you name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"records\"\u003ERecords\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA record holds what a document says, not what is drawn. The two are kept apart on purpose, so they can be compared. \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen records from a CSV file\u003C/strong\u003E reads a table whose first row names its columns; a row is matched to a parcel by the parcel&#039;s name. \u003Cstrong\u003ELand by designation\u003C/strong\u003E adds up every parcel by the land use and zone its record states.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHolders&#039; names are personal data. They are kept in the drawing&#039;s own file and are not sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-against-record\"\u003EDrawing against record\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe check lists, for each parcel, the drawn area, the recorded area and the difference, and says whether the difference is within the tolerance you typed. It also reports:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eparcels that share ground, with the area shared;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ecorners that lie close to a neighbor&#039;s line without being on it, which is what a common boundary drawn twice leaves behind;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ean outline that crosses itself;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea number used by more than one record, a parcel with no record, and a record with no parcel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA record that states no area is &quot;not compared&quot;. It is never shown as agreeing. The findings also go to \u003Cstrong\u003EProblems\u003C/strong\u003E. The check compares. It does not say which figure is right: that is for a licensed surveyor and the land registry.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-parcels\"\u003EChange parcels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick the parcels, choose the change and press \u003Cstrong\u003EWork it out\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut one parcel into equal lots\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003Ecut lots of given areas off\u003C/strong\u003E, by parallel lines in a direction you give in degrees clockwise from north.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut one parcel by a line\u003C/strong\u003E through two points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMake adjoining parcels one\u003C/strong\u003E. They must share a side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove the line between two parcels\u003C/strong\u003E to a line through two points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChange the land use or zone\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result is shown with the parcels before and after and a balance of ground, which should be nothing. \u003Cstrong\u003EYour lot rules\u003C/strong\u003E are limits you type yourself (the smallest lot, the shortest street frontage); new lots are checked against them. \u003Cstrong\u003EKeep as a proposal\u003C/strong\u003E stores it with the drawing. \u003Cstrong\u003EApply to the drawing\u003C/strong\u003E replaces the old parcels with the new ones, each with a record that names what it came from, as one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"buildings-and-quantities\"\u003EBuildings and quantities\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003EBuildings and quantities\u003C/strong\u003E. The building masses of the drawing (drawn in Massing) are laid over the parcels: each lot shows its footprint, floor area, coverage and floor area ratio. A building that crosses a lot line is counted on the lot that holds most of it, for the part that is on it, and is flagged. Below, the takeoff lists each lot&#039;s area, outline and corners and the boundary on the ground, with a line two lots share counted once. \u003Cstrong\u003ESave the quantities for TakeoffCraft\u003C/strong\u003E writes a quantities file TakeoffCraft opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"buildings-from-an-ifc-model\"\u003EBuildings from an IFC model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EBuildings and quantities\u003C/strong\u003E, press \u003Cstrong\u003ERead buildings from an IFC file\u003C/strong\u003E and choose a model from any BIM program. Each building&#039;s outline is traced and laid over the parcels, with its storeys, height and floor area. A file with a map position lands where it belongs. For any other, type how far to move it east and north and how much to turn it before you read it. \u003Cstrong\u003EMake building masses\u003C/strong\u003E turns a model&#039;s buildings into masses of the drawing, so they show on the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"notes-on-the-plan\"\u003ENotes on the plan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose a parcel in \u003Cstrong\u003ERecords\u003C/strong\u003E, open \u003Cstrong\u003ENotes on the plan\u003C/strong\u003E, write a few words and press \u003Cstrong\u003EPin the note\u003C/strong\u003E. The note is one of the drawing&#039;s issues: it shows on the plan and in the Issues list, and goes out in a BCF file. \u003Cstrong\u003EMake notes from the last check\u003C/strong\u003E pins one note for each warning of the check. Notes that are still open are printed in the papers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mark-up-in-markupcraft\"\u003EMark up in MarkupCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ENotes on the plan\u003C/strong\u003E, press \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen the plan in MarkupCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. The parcel plan opens there as a PDF at a round scale, and the open notes are saved as a BCF file. In MarkupCraft choose the BCF import and pick that file: each note lands on its spot. Mark up as usual. To bring the result home, export BCF from MarkupCraft&#039;s Markups list, then press \u003Cstrong\u003ERead marked-up notes back (BCF)\u003C/strong\u003E here. A note still at its place takes its new status and comments. A note drawn on the sheet becomes a note on the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-rules\"\u003EPlace rules\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe add-on holds no place&#039;s rules. Add the rules add-on for your place in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E (their names start with &quot;Land rules&quot;), then choose it under \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace rules\u003C/strong\u003E. You see the minimum lot sizes its texts print for each zone, what its law states about changing a parcel, and each source with a link. Type the zone in each parcel&#039;s record, then press \u003Cstrong\u003ECompare the lots with these rules\u003C/strong\u003E. Width and depth are compared only for a lot whose zoning names its street line. Anything that could not be compared is listed with the reason. A proposal is compared the same way as you draft it, and the place&#039;s worksheets appear under \u003Cstrong\u003EDocuments\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe figures are a reading aid. Check the source before you rely on one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"documents\"\u003EDocuments\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose a kept proposal in the list under \u003Cstrong\u003EChange parcels\u003C/strong\u003E, then under \u003Cstrong\u003EDocuments\u003C/strong\u003E choose a paper and press \u003Cstrong\u003EFill the paper\u003C/strong\u003E. The plain outlines that come with the add-on belong to no authority. A value nobody stated prints as &quot;[not stated: …]&quot; and is listed, so nothing is made up. Save the page and print it, or save it as PDF from the print window. An authority&#039;s own wording can be opened as a template pack.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is a filing and checking aid. It does not survey, it does not decide a boundary, and it ships no place&#039;s lot sizes, tolerances or forms.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Switch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons, then open the Land records tab. Open Records. Every parcel in the drawing is listed with its drawn area. Click a parcel, type what the register says about it (its number, the holder, the land use and zone, the area the record states and where that figure comes from), and press Add the record. Open Drawing against record and press Check the drawing against the records. Open Change parcels to work out a subdivision, a consolidation, a moved boundary or a change of land use or zone as a proposal. Open Documents to fill an application or amendment outline from a proposal. Parcels come from CivilCraft's own Parcel tool, from deed calls, from a traverse, from a field walk (see the other topic), or from a GIS polygon layer: under Records, Parcels and records from a GIS layer makes a parcel and a record from each polygon, taking the number, holder, land use, zone and area from the fields you name. Records A record holds what a document says, not what is drawn. The two are kept apart on purpose, so they can be compared. Open records from a CSV file reads a table whose first row names its columns; a row is matched to a parcel by the parcel's name. Land by designation adds up every parcel by the land use and zone its record states. Holders' names are personal data. They are kept in the drawing's own file and are not sent anywhere. Drawing against record The check lists, for each parcel, the drawn area, the recorded area and the difference, and says whether the difference is within the tolerance you typed. It also reports: parcels that share ground, with the area shared; corners that lie close to a neighbor's line without being on it, which is what a common boundary drawn twice leaves behind; an outline that crosses itself; a number used by more than one record, a parcel with no record, and a record with no parcel. A record that states no area is \"not compared\". It is never shown as agreeing. The findings also go to Problems. The check compares. It does not say which figure is right: that is for a licensed surveyor and the land registry. Change parcels Tick the parcels, choose the change and press Work it out: Cut one parcel into equal lots, or cut lots of given areas off, by parallel lines in a direction you give in degrees clockwise from north. Cut one parcel by a line through two points. Make adjoining parcels one. They must share a side. Move the line between two parcels to a line through two points. Change the land use or zone. The result is shown with the parcels before and after and a balance of ground, which should be nothing. Your lot rules are limits you type yourself (the smallest lot, the shortest street frontage); new lots are checked against them. Keep as a proposal stores it with the drawing. Apply to the drawing replaces the old parcels with the new ones, each with a record that names what it came from, as one undo step. Buildings and quantities Open Buildings and quantities. The building masses of the drawing (drawn in Massing) are laid over the parcels: each lot shows its footprint, floor area, coverage and floor area ratio. A building that crosses a lot line is counted on the lot that holds most of it, for the part that is on it, and is flagged. Below, the takeoff lists each lot's area, outline and corners and the boundary on the ground, with a line two lots share counted once. Save the quantities for TakeoffCraft writes a quantities file TakeoffCraft opens. Buildings from an IFC model Under Buildings and quantities, press Read buildings from an IFC file and choose a model from any BIM program. Each building's outline is traced and laid over the parcels, with its storeys, height and floor area. A file with a map position lands where it belongs. For any other, type how far to move it east and north and how much to turn it before you read it. Make building masses turns a model's buildings into masses of the drawing, so they show on the plan. Notes on the plan Choose a parcel in Records, open Notes on the plan, write a few words and press Pin the note. The note is one of the drawing's issues: it shows on the plan and in the Issues list, and goes out in a BCF file. Make notes from the last check pins one note for each warning of the check. Notes that are still open are printed in the papers. Mark up in MarkupCraft Under Notes on the plan, press Open the plan in MarkupCraft. The parcel plan opens there as a PDF at a round scale, and the open notes are saved as a BCF file. In MarkupCraft choose the BCF import and pick that file: each note lands on its spot. Mark up as usual. To bring the result home, export BCF from MarkupCraft's Markups list, then press Read marked-up notes back (BCF) here. A note still at its place takes its new status and comments. A note drawn on the sheet becomes a note on the plan. Place rules The add-on holds no place's rules. Add the rules add-on for your place in Settings › Add-ons (their names start with \"Land rules\"), then choose it under Place rules. You see the minimum lot sizes its texts print for each zone, what its law states about changing a parcel, and each source with a link. Type the zone in each parcel's record, then press Compare the lots with these rules. Width and depth are compared only for a lot whose zoning names its street line. Anything that could not be compared is listed with the reason. A proposal is compared the same way as you draft it, and the place's worksheets appear under Documents. The figures are a reading aid. Check the source before you rely on one. Documents Choose a kept proposal in the list under Change parcels, then under Documents choose a paper and press Fill the paper. The plain outlines that come with the add-on belong to no authority. A value nobody stated prints as \"[not stated: …]\" and is listed, so nothing is made up. Save the page and print it, or save it as PDF from the print window. An authority's own wording can be opened as a template pack. Limits This is a filing and checking aid. It does not survey, it does not decide a boundary, and it ships no place's lot sizes, tolerances or forms."},{"slug":"field-walk","scope":"addon:land-records","title":"Field walk: sketch a plot with a phone or a watch","summary":"Walk a property, press one button at each corner, and get survey points, a parcel and an honest statement of how rough they are. A watch, a headset button or a Bluetooth clicker can press the button.","section":"start","order":2,"keywords":["field walk","walk a property","GPS","GNSS","phone","watch","smartwatch","clicker","waypoint","GPX","route","corner","plot","survey points","accuracy","boundary sketch","land survey","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-need","text":"What you need","level":2},{"id":"walk-the-land","text":"Walk the land","level":2},{"id":"press-the-button-from-a-watch-a-headset-or-a-clicker","text":"Press the button from a watch, a headset or a clicker","level":2},{"id":"a-route-recorded-elsewhere","text":"A route recorded elsewhere","level":2},{"id":"into-the-drawing","text":"Into the drawing","level":2},{"id":"how-good-is-it","text":"How good is it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-need\"\u003EWhat you need\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA phone with CivilCraft open in its browser on the drawing, with the Land records add-on switched on. The phone must be allowed to give its location to the site, and the page must be open over https.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walk-the-land\"\u003EWalk the land\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cstrong\u003ELand records\u003C/strong\u003E tab, open \u003Cstrong\u003EField walk\u003C/strong\u003E and press \u003Cstrong\u003EStart a walk\u003C/strong\u003E. The walk screen fills the phone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGo to the first corner and stand still. The top of the screen shows how close the phone thinks it is: good, fair or poor, in meters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EMark corner\u003C/strong\u003E. The phone buzzes, beeps and says the point&#039;s number, so you need not look.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalk to the next corner and press again. The sketch shows the outline so far and the area, with how much it may be off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a point that is not a corner (a well, a gate), type a name in the box before you press. Clear the box for the next corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EFinish\u003C/strong\u003E. The walk is kept with the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA press is refused when the phone reports it is off by more than your limit. Wait a moment and press again. \u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E removes the last point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"press-the-button-from-a-watch-a-headset-or-a-clicker\"\u003EPress the button from a watch, a headset or a clicker\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cstrong\u003EUse a watch, headset or clicker as the button\u003C/strong\u003E. The page then plays silence under the title &quot;Field walk&quot;, so the watch shows it as what is playing. On the watch, play, pause or next marks a corner and previous removes the last point. The same works with a headset&#039;s button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA Bluetooth page-turner or presenter needs no setting: Page Down or Enter marks a corner, Page Up removes the last one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo app is put on the watch. Whether a given watch passes its buttons to the phone&#039;s browser depends on the watch and the phone; if it does not, use the screen or a clicker.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-route-recorded-elsewhere\"\u003EA route recorded elsewhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA watch or a fitness app can often save a recorded walk as a GPX file. Press \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a walk or GPX file\u003C/strong\u003E. Waypoints become corners. A recorded route is reduced to its corners: the setting says how far the route may stray from a straight side. A GPX file does not say how accurate its points are, so no accuracy is shown for it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"into-the-drawing\"\u003EInto the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose a walk and press \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd to the drawing as points and a parcel\u003C/strong\u003E. Each point becomes a survey point, and the corners become a parcel with a record marked Walked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the drawing has a coordinate system, the walk is placed where it really is. If it has none, the walk is placed beside what is drawn, north up and at true size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-good-is-it\"\u003EHow good is it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA phone is rarely closer than a few meters, and worse under trees and beside buildings. Standing still and averaging steadies a point but does not make a phone into a survey instrument. The area is shown with a &quot;give or take&quot; worked out from the accuracy the phone reported at each corner; treat it as a guide to the size of the doubt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA walk is a sketch for planning, for a first look and for talking to a surveyor. It is not a boundary survey and must not be used as one.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you need A phone with CivilCraft open in its browser on the drawing, with the Land records add-on switched on. The phone must be allowed to give its location to the site, and the page must be open over https. Walk the land In the Land records tab, open Field walk and press Start a walk. The walk screen fills the phone. Go to the first corner and stand still. The top of the screen shows how close the phone thinks it is: good, fair or poor, in meters. Press Mark corner. The phone buzzes, beeps and says the point's number, so you need not look. Walk to the next corner and press again. The sketch shows the outline so far and the area, with how much it may be off. For a point that is not a corner (a well, a gate), type a name in the box before you press. Clear the box for the next corner. Press Finish. The walk is kept with the drawing. A press is refused when the phone reports it is off by more than your limit. Wait a moment and press again. Undo removes the last point. Press the button from a watch, a headset or a clicker Tick Use a watch, headset or clicker as the button. The page then plays silence under the title \"Field walk\", so the watch shows it as what is playing. On the watch, play, pause or next marks a corner and previous removes the last point. The same works with a headset's button. A Bluetooth page-turner or presenter needs no setting: Page Down or Enter marks a corner, Page Up removes the last one. No app is put on the watch. Whether a given watch passes its buttons to the phone's browser depends on the watch and the phone; if it does not, use the screen or a clicker. A route recorded elsewhere A watch or a fitness app can often save a recorded walk as a GPX file. Press Open a walk or GPX file. Waypoints become corners. A recorded route is reduced to its corners: the setting says how far the route may stray from a straight side. A GPX file does not say how accurate its points are, so no accuracy is shown for it. Into the drawing Choose a walk and press Add to the drawing as points and a parcel. Each point becomes a survey point, and the corners become a parcel with a record marked Walked. If the drawing has a coordinate system, the walk is placed where it really is. If it has none, the walk is placed beside what is drawn, north up and at true size. How good is it A phone is rarely closer than a few meters, and worse under trees and beside buildings. Standing still and averaging steadies a point but does not make a phone into a survey instrument. The area is shown with a \"give or take\" worked out from the accuracy the phone reported at each corner; treat it as a guide to the size of the doubt. A walk is a sketch for planning, for a first look and for talking to a surveyor. It is not a boundary survey and must not be used as one."}]};
